{
  "easy": {
    "heading": "Warming Up With Some Simple Sums",
    "body": "Today started off with a really sweet little puzzle that focused heavily on how single cells can act as anchors. I immediately looked at the region at 1,2 which had a sum target of 3. Since it was just one cell, I knew that cell had to be a 3. Looking at our dominoes, that meant I had to use either the 4,3 or the 3,5 there. I decided to try placing the 4,3 domino vertically at 0,2 and 1,2. This was a great move because the region containing 0,1 and 0,2 needed to sum to 10. Since 0,2 was now a 4, that meant 0,1 had to be a 6.\n\nFollowing that thread, I saw that 0,0 and 0,1 were a domino pair. Since 0,1 was a 6, I checked the 2,6 domino and it fit perfectly because the constraint for 0,0 was that it had to be greater than 1. A value of 2 definitely fits that bill! From there, the bottom of the board cleared up quickly. The sum 10 region at 2,3 and 2,4 was easily solved by using the 5,0 and 6,6 dominoes to fill in the remaining spots, making sure the equals constraint at 1,0 and 2,0 was satisfied by the 6,6 domino.",
  },
  "medium": {
    "heading": "Chaining Equals for a Medium Win",
    "body": "The medium puzzle today was all about those equals constraints. When you see a bunch of cells that all have to be the same value, it really limits your options. I started by looking at the three-cell equals chain at 0,2, 0,3, and 1,3. Because 0,3 and 1,3 were part of the same domino, they had to be the same number. The only way to satisfy that was to use a double domino, and the 3,3 fit the bill perfectly. This meant 0,2 also had to be a 3.\n\nOnce I had those 3s in place, I looked at the bottom right where 2,3 had to sum to 5. Since it was a single cell, it was a 5. I used the 5,3 domino across 2,3 and 3,3, which worked because 3,3 was part of another equals region with 3,2. It felt like a giant game of telephone where one number told the next one what to be. The empty cells at 1,0 and 2,0 were a little intimidating at first, but they actually gave me more freedom to place the 4,0 and 1,0 dominoes to satisfy the less than 4 constraint at 3,0.",
  },
  "hard": {
    "heading": "Navigating the Deep End of the Pip Pool",
    "body": "Wow, the hard puzzle today really made me work for it! The key was definitely the sum 11 regions at the left side of the board. Since the highest pip value is 6, a sum of 11 can only be made with a 5 and a 6. I spent a long time looking at the 0,0 and 1,0 region and the 2,0 and 3,0 region. By comparing these with the sum 5 region at 4,0 and 5,0, I realized that the dominoes had to be placed very specifically to avoid running out of 5s and 6s.\n\nThe real breakthrough happened when I looked at the single cell region at 2,2 which required a sum of 6. That meant the cell was a 6. I paired it with the 2,3 cell using the 3,6 domino. This was huge because it set off a chain reaction for the equals regions in the middle of the grid. Because 2,3, 2,4, and 2,5 were all equal, they all became 3s. This forced the 3,4 and 2,4 domino to be the 4,3 pair. It took a bit of back-and-forth, especially around the sum 10 region at the bottom left, but once those 3s were locked in, the rest of the board finally behaved.",
  },
  "tips": "Always look for single-cell regions first because they tell you exactly what number goes in that spot. After that, focus on the largest sums like 11 or 12 or the smallest ones like 1 or 2, as they have the fewest possible combinations. If you get stuck, look for the equals regions that span across two different dominoes, as these create a logic chain that can solve multiple areas at once.",
  "learned": "Today I really appreciated how helpful empty regions can be. At first, they seem like they provide no information, but they actually act as the flexible joints of the puzzle that allow the more constrained regions to work. I also noticed that in the hard puzzle, the sum 11 regions were placed in a way that forced me to use my 5s and 6s early, which is a classic Ian Livengood and Rodolfo Kurchan trick to make you think ahead.",
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What does an empty region mean in NYT Pips?",
      "answer": "An empty region means there are no specific mathematical constraints on that cell. You can place any pip value there as long as it is part of a valid domino and doesn't break any rules in neighboring regions."
    },
    {
      "question": "How do equals regions work when they cover multiple dominoes?",
      "answer": "Every cell within a region marked with an equals sign must have the exact same number of pips. If the region covers parts of three different dominoes, all three of those cells must match."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I use the same domino twice in one puzzle?",
      "answer": "No, each domino in the provided list for the day is unique and can only be used once to fill the grid."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the best way to start a hard Pips puzzle?",
      "answer": "Start by identifying the most restrictive regions. These are usually single-cell regions or very high/low sums. Once you place those, look for how they limit the options for the dominoes that must connect to them."
    },
    {
      "question": "Are dominoes allowed to be placed diagonally?",
      "answer": "No, dominoes must always be placed either horizontally or vertically, covering two adjacent cells."
    }
  ]
